{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Neither shall he multiply wives to himself.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory explained: no more than eighteen, corresponding to the number of wives that David had. Of these, six are explicitly named in Scripture, and it is written ״ואם מעט ואסופה לך״ — and if they are few, gathered to you — like this and like this, six and six; that makes eighteen. And they explained the reason: ״ולא יסור לבבו״ — that his heart not turn away — because a woman may seduce a man to turn aside from the fear of HaShem, blessed be He, for so did the mother of all living do, even if the women were few." "<b>And silver and gold he shall not multiply to himself exceedingly.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory explained: only enough to pay the apsania, that is, to pay the wages of his soldiers year by year, those who go out and come in with him every single year; but he is not permitted to make of it a treasury or a treasure. And the reason is לבלתי רום לבבו, lest his heart be lifted up, for this leads to לבלתי רום לבבו מאחיו — lest his heart be lifted up above his brethren. And with this He gave a reason for all three: in the multiplication of horses — lest he bring the people back; in the multiplication of wives — ולא יסור לבבו; in the multiplication of silver — לבלתי רום לבבו, for when he makes a treasury of silver and gold he will trust in his treasure and remove his trust from YHWH, blessed be He, and it is fitting for a king that he place HaShem as his trust and that the fear of HaShem be his treasure and his fortress, as it is written (Job 24:25): ״ושית על עפר בצר ובצור נחלים אופיר, והיה שדי בצריך וכסף תועפות לך.״" "<b>And the wise.</b> R. Abraham of blessed memory gave a reason for silver and gold he shall not multiply: lest he burden Israel with the weight of the tax, for so we find with Solomon, who burdened Israel with the yoke of the tax on account of the vast quantity of silver and gold that he had. For wealth is likened to fire: the more wood you add, the more the flame grows. And so we find that all Israel complained against Solomon to Rehoboam his son, as they said to him (1 Kings 12:4–11): ״אביך הכביד את עולנו״ — Your father made our yoke heavy — and the matter escalated until they killed Adoniram, who was the officer over the tax, and stoned him in his house. And we find Solomon, who was the greatest of the world, yet stumbled in these three: he multiplied horses, as it is said (1 Kings 5:6), ״ויהי לשלמה ארבעים אלף ארוות סוסים״ — and Solomon had forty thousand stalls of horses; he multiplied wives, as it is said (1 Kings 11:3), ״ויהי לו נשים שרות שבע מאות ופילגשים שלש מאות״ — and he had seven hundred princess wives and three hundred concubines; and likewise silver, as it is said (1 Kings 10:27), ״ויתן המלך את הכסף בירושלים כאבנים״ — and the king made silver in Jerusalem as stones, and it is written (2 Chronicles 9:20), ״אין כסף לא נחשב בימי שלמה למאומה״ — silver was not counted as anything in the days of Solomon. He said concerning all of them: I shall multiply and not be bound, and he relied on his wisdom to stand against the Torah, and he stumbled through them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:49:14.032020842Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}